# Custom IoC rule: outbound traffic from this network to a Spamhaus DROP-listed
# (hijacked/malicious) netblock -- e.g. a compromised local device beaconing to C2.
# `et/open` already bundles ~80 "ET DROP Spamhaus DROP Listed Traffic Inbound group N"
# rules covering this same list, but scoped `any -> $HOME_NET any` -- INBOUND only
# (confirmed by reading the live compiled ruleset). This rule covers the direction
# that leaves uncovered: HOME_NET -> a DROP-listed destination.
#
# Suricata's `dataset` feature does NOT support CIDR ranges for `type ip` (confirmed
# empirically: exact IPs load fine, a /24 fails with "dataset data parse failed" --
# this is undocumented and was not assumed). Spamhaus DROP is ~1,670 CIDR ranges, not
# individual IPs, so `dataset` is the wrong tool here despite fitting the domain rule
# above. Suricata's IP Reputation (`iprep`) feature is built for exactly this
# (network-based reputation scoring) and does accept CIDR entries in its reputation
# list file (also confirmed empirically). Category/list files generated by
# scripts/refresh-ioc-datasets.sh; the reputation-categories-file/default-reputation-path/
# reputation-files block enabling this is in suricata.yaml.
#
# Custom sid range 9500000-9599999, clear of et/open (2000000s) and abuse.ch feed sids.
alert ip $HOME_NET any -> any any (msg:"IOC Spamhaus DROP-listed destination (outbound)"; iprep:dst,SpamhausDROP,>,0; classtype:trojan-activity; sid:9500002; rev:1; metadata:created_at 2026_07_26;)
